# Define the behavior

Write or update the business requirements of the features this task touches: Gherkin rules with
stable IDs plus a glossary, readable by every stakeholder. Once accepted they are the source of
truth that implementation and Dev QA check against, so state business outcomes, never UI steps or
technology.

## Inputs

- The brief: the original request (context only), `Class`, `Modifiers`, `Route`, `Context`,
  `Features` (prefix, new or existing, coverage), and the latest open `## Rework` entry when Tech
  design or Dev QA sent `REQUIREMENTS_GAP` or Implement sent `REVISE_REQUIREMENTS`.
- `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/README.md` (index), `GLOSSARY.md`, each affected feature's
  `{{vars.requirementsFile}}`, and its `{{vars.techDesignFile}}` (for the no-technical-impact check).
- Adoption, or coverage partial or none: the code, tests and existing docs of the touched behavior
  (old specs such as `docs/specs`, PRDs, `.feature` files, test names).

## Procedure

1. **Check the route.** Continue when `Route:` is `full` or `behavior`. Reached on a `design` or
   `code` route through a Rework entry: propose the corrected class and route (for example Bug fix /
   requirements improved), and once the user confirms, update the header as the Task brief section
   says and continue; go to step 11 only when that needs a full classification (other features, a
   split, out of scope). Otherwise stop and tell the user which step the route runs next.
   Re-entered: address the open items you own in the latest Rework entry; keep accepted work.
2. **Bootstrap missing structure** from the
   [requirements skeleton](references/requirements-skeleton.md): greenfield, or no
   `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/` yet → the folder, `README.md` and `GLOSSARY.md`. A new feature → its
   folder, `{{vars.requirementsFile}}` and index row, with the prefix from the brief; if the index
   already uses that prefix, agree another one with the user and correct the brief's `Features:`.
3. **Baseline first** (adoption, or coverage partial or none): describe only the touched behavior
   and the rules it depends on, from existing docs and test names before code. These extracted
   rules form their own group in the summary, the user confirms each one and step 10 records their
   IDs. Current behavior that looks wrong becomes a question whose default answer is "follow-up":
   leave it out of the rules and add it under **Follow-ups and known gaps** in
   `## Hand-off to the QA team`.
4. **Write the change** in the [Gherkin style](references/gherkin-style.md) (open it before the
   first scenario) and the file layout of the skeleton, in the language the existing requirements
   use (English by default):

   | Outcome | Edit | IDs |
   |---|---|---|
   | New requirements | new Rules and Scenarios | next free numbers |
   | Requirements changed | only the affected Rules and Scenarios | kept while it answers the same business question (new wording or a new answer); retired and replaced for another rule, a split or a merge |
   | Requirements clarified | wording, glossary, added scenarios that pin existing behavior for a silent case; no outcome of an existing scenario changes | existing kept; an added scenario gets the next number |
   | Requirements improved (bug) | only the case the bug exposed | a corrected scenario keeps its ID; an added one gets the next number |

   Before writing, list per rule its boundary (at and just past a limit), what it rejects, who may
   act, and the business outcome of a failure. Ask the user about gaps only, at most 3 questions at
   a time, each with options and a recommended default; never invent a rule. An edit the class does
   not allow (a clarification that changes an outcome, a bug fix that rewrites other cases) means
   the class is wrong: correct the header with the user as in step 1, or step 11. Under `revert`,
   undo the reverted change's requirement edits and retire the IDs it added. Retired IDs go to the
   file's `## Retired IDs` list; numbers are never reused.
5. **Glossary.** The local glossary lists every domain term its file uses: Term | Kind (subject,
   object, event, state, value) | Meaning | Avoid. A term used by several features lives in
   `GLOSSARY.md`; local rows copy its Meaning word for word and link to it. When a second feature
   starts using a local term, move it to `GLOSSARY.md` and turn both rows into copies. Name every
   other feature file you touch, and every feature a changed shared Meaning affects, in the summary.
6. **Leakage check** on every file you edited. Failures, fix them: ticket keys (a key whose prefix
   is not in the index), requester names, sentences copied from `## Original request`. Warnings,
   each with a proposed fix or "keep" for the summary: change-relative words (new, now, no longer,
   currently, will) and the flagged vocabulary of the Gherkin style. Every sentence must read
   correctly to someone who never saw the request.
7. **No-technical-impact check.** For each added (not extracted), changed or retired ID: does it
   change persisted data, an interface or integration, UI placement, or a target in the quality
   catalog? Also search the feature's `{{vars.techDesignFile}}` for these IDs: a row citing one is a hit.
   - Any hit, or the `ui-design` modifier → `NEXT`: set `Route: full` and list the hit sections in
     `Tech design sections:` with the keys of the Task brief section (`data`, `interfaces`, `ui`,
     `quality (<codes>)`).
   - No hit → `NO_TECH_IMPACT`: set `Route: behavior` and `Tech design sections: none`. It is a
     forward event: no Rework entry.
   - A route switch (full ↔ behavior) also gets one dated line, with the hits or their absence, in
     the reclassification log of `## Classification`. Once the brief records an accepted tech
     design, `Route: full` stays: the check only decides whether this revision needs it again.
8. **Gate checklist.** Every item holds before you present the summary:
   - [ ] IDs well formed (`<PREFIX>-<n>`, `<PREFIX>-<n>.<m>`, prefix in the index), unique, never
     reused: a new number is the highest in the file, Retired IDs included, plus 1.
   - [ ] Every Rule has at least one Scenario, plus a negative one when it rejects, forbids or
     limits something; every Rule and Scenario passes the checklists of the Gherkin style.
   - [ ] Every domain term resolves to exactly one glossary row; shared copies match `GLOSSARY.md`.
   - [ ] No two rules contradict each other, in the file or with the rules it cites.
   - [ ] No leakage failure; every warning has a proposed resolution.
   - [ ] Index row, `Coverage:` and `Related rules:` lines current.
9. **Get acceptance.** Present the IDs added, changed, reworded, retired and extracted (each with
   its name), glossary changes, warnings with your proposals, follow-ups, and the event with its
   tech design sections. Wait for an explicit yes in this conversation; silence or approval of one part is not
   acceptance. On any change: edit, rerun steps 5 to 8, present again. Iterate here until accepted.
10. **Commit.** Stage only the requirement files you changed and commit them alone:
    `docs(req): <PREFIX> requirements for <task>` (several features: `docs(req): CHK, ACC
    requirements for <task>`). Write its sha into the header (`Accepted: … requirements <sha>`,
    replacing an older one); when step 3 extracted rules, append their IDs, keeping those listed
    before: `requirements <sha> (extracted: CHK-1, CHK-1.1)`. Mark the Rework items done, commit
    the brief: `docs(task): requirements accepted for <task>`. Then fire the event chosen in step 7.
11. **Reclassify** when the class or route no longer fits (steps 1 and 4) or the task turns out to
    be out of scope: tell the user why and get agreement, append a Rework entry (`RECLASSIFY`,
    define-behavior, numbered: what you found and the class you suggest), commit only the brief
    (`docs(task): reclassify <task>`), name the requirement drafts left uncommitted, fire
    `RECLASSIFY`.

## Outputs

- `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/<feature>/{{vars.requirementsFile}}` for every feature of the brief;
  `README.md` and `GLOSSARY.md` when created or changed.
- Brief: `Route:`, `Tech design sections:` and `Accepted: … requirements <sha>` (with the extracted
  IDs) in the header; Rework items marked done; a reclassification log line when the route
  switched; follow-ups in the hand-off.
- Two commits: the requirement files alone, then the brief.

## Exit criteria

- `NEXT` or `NO_TECH_IMPACT`: the gate checklist passes and the user explicitly accepted the
  summary, warnings included, in this conversation; the requirements commit holds only
  requirement files, its sha is in the committed brief, and the tree is clean.
- The event matches step 7: `NEXT` with the sections listed; `NO_TECH_IMPACT` only with no hit and
  no `ui-design`.
- `RECLASSIFY`: the user agreed and the Rework entry is committed in the brief.

## Pitfalls

- UI scripts or technical design in rules (screens, endpoints, tables, response times): placement
  and metrics belong to the tech design, everything else to the code.
- Request leakage: story narratives, "the client asked", ticket keys, solution wording.
- Baselining the whole feature, or writing a suspected bug down as a rule.
- Renumbering, reusing or deleting IDs instead of retiring them.
- Choosing `NO_TECH_IMPACT` to save a step: one hit means the route includes Tech design.
- Rules nobody confirmed: ideas of your own are questions in the summary, not requirements.
- Editing code or tests here; committing requirements together with the brief.

